 started college I was  doing GIS a back then it was on a VAX I  was taking Landsat imagery I was doing  vegetation stress indexes trying to  figure out what to do I was mapping spot  all that spotted owl habitat marbled  murrelet habitat up in the north coast  and so I early early on in my in my life  and in my career I was convinced that  taking data and applying it good  and applying it in a way where you could  visualize it and get people to relate to  what was going on around them in a  personal level on a local level was  really what helped us make good  decisions as a community as a society  and drive change and so that was really  how I kind of started my whole journey  down this path of really believing that  so I got out of college I was going to  be an engineer but it turned out I  didn't really love calculus and fluid  dynamics so I I went more the natural  side and did a lot of hydrology and  things like that but I got out of  college and it was during the Reagan era  there was no jobs in the environmental  area goes go figure  and I got hired by a county because I  knew how to set up computers so that's  how I got into computers because I was  setting up all the computers in college  and really it took off from there so I  tell everybody if you want to be the CIO  of a government someday go to Humboldt  State you can actually sources planning  and you'll be set your your on the track  to be a world-famous CIO someday in  government but that fast-forward now 25  years later 25 years later to where we  are today you know in San Mateo County  if you think about our county you know  we're 800 square miles well 500 sorry  500 square miles about 800,000 people in  our county for those of you that aren't  from around here we're kind of the  Forgotten County between San Francisco  and San Mateo so our County actually  goes all the way from the southern  border of San Francisco which is about  50 square miles everything south of  there matter of fact San Francisco  airports in my County they pay us for an  INT which we love and goes all the way  down to Palo Alto we get the poor side  we get East Palo Alto they get stand for  down in Santa Clara but on the coast  side that goes all the way down almost  the Santa Cruz so we have a very diverse  County you know on on what we call the  Bayside the 101 corridor you know you  have 22 different cities all down that  corridor just amazing amount of urban  really dense probably a lot of you that  work here in the barrier probably your  CEOs or founders probably live in my  County Woodside Atherton Menlo Park  those are all in my County so you know  we have this very interesting very dense  very technically savvy engaged  population now if you go  over 280 if you've been down in our  County and you go to the Westside it's  all agriculture all mountainous all very  different now early on in the  presentation you saw a slide showing  where the sensory locations are if you  think it's challenging for San Francisco  which is 50 square miles so I have one  sensor in there County we're five  hundred square miles and I have one  sensor in my County and it's on the bay  side in Redwood City so if you live in  Half Moon Bay which is on the coast and  you're trying to understand what the air  quality's like in your neighborhood  based on a sensor in Redwood City you're  probably not going to see a really good  correlation between your real-world  experience and the stuff you're seeing  as the official air quality index so  that's how we got engaged here with  Google and acumen geotab cuz we were  convinced in the county that we needed  to engage partners like them to help  really understand what was going on with  our environment to make things better  for our communities at a local level so  we launched about a year ago now an  initiative in our County called SMC labs  government as some of you may know isn't  known for how fast we move so we created  this innovation group they engaged with  our partners we have signed an agreement  with them which we're very excited about  we see this is the way to really start  understanding given the fact we have 22  cities we have a huge fleet of vehicles  we manage you know over a thousand  vehicles for Public Safety for Public  Health  we manage Sam trans vehicles that do the  bus routes all over our County we think  this is a great opportunity to use a  combination of geotab and a clamed Atta  to really start understanding what's  going on in our county because you know  these problems we're trying to solve in  the real world they're not specific to  an invisible political boundary you know  the problems around air quality don't  stop when you crawl from one city to  another when they're all together  there's really a regional problem we're  trying to solve we're trying to engage  on all of us probably have loved ones or  people we care about in schools when we  when we head to fires  one of my other part-time jobs I'm a  trustee on a school board but when we  were having the fires up in the North  Bay that wasn't it just a North Bay  granted I mean that was horrific for the  people up in the North Bay but the huge  plume of smoke went offshore because of  the wind currents and came in over our  County and it was really eye-opening  just to see impactful that was to how  people perceived their quality of life  and things like that there was people  calling our hospitals sheltering in  place schools were closing I mean it  really caused sort of this ancillary  impact to the community to our our whole  region for a while that I think you know  you're always concerned about the people  who's losing their houses who's losing  their lives but it really brought home  to all of us how we're all in this  together and we have to solve these  problems together and air quality's just  I think one of those really easy to  understand important values that we can  look at that we can track and I think  you know my hope is in the coming months  as we continue to explore and expand  this partnership with Google and with a  Clemmie and geo tab and we start to  deploy more of this technology as we  start to do some of this analysis as we  create these great interactive maps and  visualizations that we can share with  politicians and the community about what  the current condition is in their  neighborhoods and what they can do in  policies and actions we can take based  on data that that's really gonna start  helping us make changes in our  communities here in the Bay Area and  then you know launch to other places I  know this is already going on we're just  in Denver last week at a smart cities  conference and they're talking about it  I think this is just really the tip of  the iceberg we're in the few years this  is just going to be the norm for  everything so I really appreciate you  all being here you're the brains behind  all this and I'm gonna hand it back over  to Chad  all righty so I wanted to first off  thank you all for for going with us on  the journey yeah that we've handed the  mic off a whole bunch of times in the  handbook for to presenting at this  conference they say not to do that but  really the story spanned a whole bunch  of organizations and a whole bunch of  use case and it was really important to  make sure to bring John on so that he  could explain like who the people are  that benefit from the maps that Mike and  divita are making so thank you very much  for coming  [Music]

